<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n49611" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ūbĕro</span></b> ,
		āre, v. a. and n.
		2. uber. * <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Neutr.</i>, <i>to be fruitful</i> or
				<i>productive</i>, <i>to bear
			fruit</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">neque enim olea continuo biennio uberat,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Col.
					5, 9, 11</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Act.</i>, <i>to make fruitful</i>,
				<i>to fertilize</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">hoc velut coitu steriles arbores uberantur,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Pall. Oct. 8, 3</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">id. Febr. 17, 4</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>